body:
@samdark 

After some more research, it appears that the use of the Location header for a 202 response is non-standard.  There is nothing in any RFCs that I could find on the subject that would suggest using a Location header for a 202 response.  On the other hand, none of them, in any way, suggest that it should not be used. There are several forum replies and the "http://restcookbook.com/" recommends it, but I don't at this time think this is a bug.  Sorry for any time wasted or headaches about this.  

That said, it would be nice to be able to use a Location header with a 202 response.  The current sendHeaders method does not allow for that.  I would suggest having some way to 'force' the status code for a response, possibly a property of the Yii::$app->response instance.

@nd4c Currently, even if you do

``` php
header("Location: http://example.com/", true, 302);
return;
```

in controller, Yii will overwrite the status code with 200 in `sendHeaders()` method, so it doesn't change anything. For `redirect()` method, the default is 302, so it doesn't change anything either.

With fix you can now do

``` php
return $this->redirect('http://example.com/', 202);
```

or

``` php
Yii::$app->response->headers->add("Location", "http://example.com");
Yii::$app->response->statusCode = 202;
```

Let me know if you have any doubts, or better wording for changelog.

// Also, `Response::$isRedirection` will of course still return false for 202. Basically what the dev said in the php bug report is how I see Response object: It's mainly for http response. With the fix developer is now free to set any status code he wants in combination with Location-redirect, but he should then not rely on `$isRedirection`...
